  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096669,
      "id": "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af",
      "title": "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a3908110-287a-5810-aa0b-9be9ba1f51be",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=4.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(4)=-8.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-8-(-4)=-4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=-4$ and ${x}_{3}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-4)+(4)=7.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=7-(4)-(4)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=-1$, ${x}_{2}=-4$, and ${x}_{3}=4.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨-1,-4,4⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "20011c86-58a2-579a-ba9b-a43048c1c632",
