    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e9e4a49f-ccf6-5104-96de-1d4a4a1995b6",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-3)=-2.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-2-(-3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(1)-(-3)=4.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4-(-1)-(3)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨2,1,-3⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ac1be0a0-a99d-5383-83b5-086e6303a811",
